# Every text file checks out with LF line endings on every platform, Windows included (0.17.3).
#
# Why: git on Windows defaults to converting LF to CRLF on checkout. A CRLF shell script fails
# under bash ("$'\r': command not found"), and a converted file no longer matches the
# SHA256SUMS a published tree carries, so install.sh would flag every file of an honest
# download. `text=auto` leaves binaries (PDFs, images, zips) untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except Windows batch files (0.19.2): cmd.exe misparses labels and blocks in an LF .cmd, so
# install.cmd is CRLF in the repository's working tree on every platform -- and so in gt-src
# and in SHA256SUMS, which are made from that tree.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
